                <pb facs="1152" n="1028" xml:id="pg1028"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">767.68119/651: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Special Mission at Lausanne to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lausanne</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-06-28T12:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 28,
                                1923—12 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received June 29—7:43
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>470. Our telegram 465 of June 27. The instructions received by Pellé have not
                    served, as expected, to clear the atmosphere. In fact the contrary is the case.
                    The French Government favors sending Ismet a definite ultimatum which does not
                    present any material concession by the Allies. It is the desire of the British
                    and Italians to avoid extreme measures and to investigate every possibility for
                    a compromise. Late tonight Ismet is having separate conferences with Montagna
                    and Rumbold. The barometer of the conference, which is sensitive and rapidly
                    varying, has receded considerably from the high point attained the day before
                    yesterday.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
